WEBVTT

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:07.980
Commodore Yang! Commodore Yang!

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:10.190
Julian Mintz

00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:10.180
Commodore Yang, you'll be late!

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:14.150
Late for what?

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:15.870
Commodore Yang!

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:20.870
What a way to start your morning.

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:25.320
It's unbelievable that you, of all people, 
would be late to a memorial service.

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:27.440
I see...

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:29.750
Yeah, you're right.

00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:37.040
Julian, what happened to those books? 
The ones from the box I hadn't unpacked?

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.300
I already put them away.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:41.860
Then how about the Wanli porcelain 
from the box beside it?

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:44.350
I put them in the place 
where they look best.

00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:46.840
Take care.

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:48.640
Hey, Julian...

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:49.790
Yes?

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:52.950
Sorry to have you do so much.

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:55.770
I'll act more like a real 
guardian when I get back.

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:57.100
See you.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:01.410
Commodore Yang!

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:32.000
<b>Legend of the Galactic Heroes</b>

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.510
Capital Planet Heinessen

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:41.430
Free Planets Alliance Navy
Integrated Tactical Operations Center

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:49.890
Looks like you're not totally awake yet,

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:52.510
hero of Astarte.

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:54.320
<u>Alliance Navy Integrated Tactical Operations Center Deputy Adjutant General
</u>Rear Admiral Alex Cazerne

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:56.380
Who's a hero?

00:02:56.380 --> 00:02:58.890
The guy standing right in front of me.

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:02.020
That's what all the press has 
been writing about you.

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:05.120
I'm the leader of a losing army.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:08.260
Yes, the Alliance Navy lost.

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:11.660
That's exactly why we need a hero.

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:14.570
There'd be no need for heroes if we'd won.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:20.070
After a defeat, you have to take the 
people's focus away from what's going on.

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:22.580
That's how it was at El Facil, right?

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:26.930
By the way... why are you all wet?

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:28.540
Oh, er...

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:33.770
<b>Episode 5</b>

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:33.770
<b>Birth of the 13th Fleet</b>

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:44.400
To all citizens and soldiers in attendance:

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:48.900
for what purpose have we 
gathered here on this day?

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:55.120
To console the spirits of the 1,500,000 heroes 
who died in the battle at the Astarte Starzone.

00:03:55.830 --> 00:04:01.720
They nobly gave their lives to defend 
the freedom and peace of our homeland,

00:03:56.210 --> 00:04:01.380
<u>Alliance Central Government National Defense Committee Chairman
</u>Job Trunicht

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:04.210
and to tell us, those left behind,

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:11.100
that there was something more 
precious than their lives.

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:13.090
What is that something?

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:16.390
It is our homeland, and our freedom!

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:19.850
Why did those 1,500,000 servicemen die?

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:23.910
Because their leaders couldn't 
command them well enough.

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:29.030
They laid down their lives to defend 
our homeland, and our freedom!

00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:34.630
I would like to make this abundantly clear!

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:40.620
Our homeland and our freedom are 
worthy of defending with our lives.

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:45.010
You pacifists among us who advocate 
for reconciliation with the Empire,

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:47.340
awaken from your delusions!

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:50.960
Peace does not come as easily 
as you profess it will!

00:04:52.130 --> 00:04:53.420
<i>I don't know...</i>

00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:57.440
<i>I'd say it's just as easy to warmonger 
from within your nice, safe haven.</i>

00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:04.100
Those who oppose this battle to oppose the 
Galactic Empire's autocratic totalitarianism...

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:07.320
have forsaken their homeland!

00:05:08.710 --> 00:05:11.270
We know the history.

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:14.530
Our homeland, with its great history!

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:17.130
Our homeland, with its freedom!

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:20.970
We must stand and fight to defend...

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:23.580
the one thing worth defending!

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:26.870
Now is the time to fight for our homeland!

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:30.450
Long live the Alliance! 
Long live our republic!

00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:32.570
Defeat the Empire!

00:05:32.570 --> 00:05:36.470
Long live the Alliance! 
Long live our republic!

00:05:36.470 --> 00:05:38.610
Defeat the Empire!

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:42.470
Long live the Alliance! 
Long live our republic!

00:05:42.470 --> 00:05:44.790
Defeat the Empire!

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:48.550
Long live the Alliance! 
Long live our republic!

00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:50.740
Defeat the Empire!

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:53.190
Why aren't you standing?

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:53.200
Captain Christian

00:05:53.650 --> 00:05:56.120
This is a free country.

00:05:56.120 --> 00:05:59.970
We have the freedom to not 
stand if we don't want to.

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:02.810
I'm only exercising that freedom.

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:05.390
Then why don't you want to stand?

00:06:06.350 --> 00:06:08.840
I exercise my freedom 
to withhold my answer.

00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:11.010
What are you trying to—

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:12.110
Christian.

00:06:26.650 --> 00:06:30.260
Ladies and gentlemen, our greatest weapon...

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:33.400
is the unity shared among us all.

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:40.390
So long as we live in a free country 
governed as a democratic republic,

00:06:40.390 --> 00:06:45.210
no objective, however noble, 
can be forced upon the people.

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:54.930
<u></u>Jessica Edwards

00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:55.190
Defense Committee Chairman, 
my name is Jessica Edwards.

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:56.820
Jessica?

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:00.540
I am the fiancée of Jean Robert Lapp,

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:04.470
an officer of the 6th Fleet who 
died in the Battle of Astarte.

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:08.120
No... I <i>was</i> his fiancée.

00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:13.960
Goodness... My deepest condolences 
to you, young lady.

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:14.570
However...

00:07:14.570 --> 00:07:17.580
I am not demanding sympathy.

00:07:18.510 --> 00:07:24.550
Chairman, my fiancé died a noble 
death in defense of our homeland.

00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:26.370
Of course...

00:07:26.810 --> 00:07:31.700
You are truly what every woman on the 
home front should aspire to be.

00:07:32.110 --> 00:07:36.760
I am certain your commendable 
spirit will be warmly rewarded.

00:07:38.380 --> 00:07:40.000
Thank you, Chairman.

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:45.780
I am here because I wish to 
ask you a single question.

00:07:46.390 --> 00:07:49.150
Ah, and what would that be?

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:52.570
Where are you at the moment?

00:07:54.630 --> 00:07:56.820
Er... Come again?

00:07:56.820 --> 00:08:01.770
My fiancé went to the battlefield 
to protect his homeland,

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:04.840
and now he is no longer in this world.

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:08.270
Chairman, where are you?

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:11.120
Where do you stand as you glorify death?

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:14.380
I gave my fiancé as a sacrifice!

00:08:14.980 --> 00:08:19.450
Where is your family as you advocate 
for the sacrifice of these people?

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:23.590
There is no fault with the 
speech you just gave,

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:26.980
but are you living by that 
same standard yourself?

00:08:27.690 --> 00:08:30.320
The young lady has lost her composure.

00:08:30.710 --> 00:08:32.280
Escort her out of the room.

00:08:33.050 --> 00:08:35.900
My speech is over. 
The national anthem, please!

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:45.240
Let's go.

00:08:45.620 --> 00:08:46.580
Yang?

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:51.040
This isn't where you should be.

00:09:17.960 --> 00:09:19.680
Jessica, hold on to something!

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:20.530
Right!

00:10:11.420 --> 00:10:13.710
I'm sorry to get you involved in this.

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:16.530
No, it's not your fault.

00:10:17.810 --> 00:10:18.790
Yang...

00:10:20.170 --> 00:10:23.810
You must have thought I was 
an awful woman, didn't you?

00:10:23.810 --> 00:10:24.970
Why?

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:29.110
Most bereaved families are enduring 
their grief in silence,

00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:33.000
and I had to go and shout such things 
in front of all those people.

00:10:33.510 --> 00:10:35.520
It makes sense that you'd be disgusted.

00:10:36.340 --> 00:10:39.100
No, I don't think that at all.

00:10:39.450 --> 00:10:42.460
I hear you were promoted, Yang.

00:10:43.030 --> 00:10:45.940
Jean Robert always had such praise for you.

00:10:45.940 --> 00:10:48.030
He said you were the pride of your class.

00:10:48.750 --> 00:10:53.430
All the admirals in our navy who aren't 
you ought to feel humiliated.

00:10:53.950 --> 00:10:57.640
They let more than a million 
people die in a single battle.

00:10:58.380 --> 00:11:00.470
It's an ethical disgrace.

00:11:00.990 --> 00:11:03.390
I don't think that's quite right.

00:11:04.430 --> 00:11:08.610
True, it might have been foolish 
to allow that many people to die.

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:14.230
But what if we'd won and caused more than 
a million deaths on the other side?

00:11:14.630 --> 00:11:17.480
Should we be praised for that?

00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:23.250
Ethics have nothing to do with 
victory or loss in war.

00:11:26.440 --> 00:11:28.480
You haven't changed at all.

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:31.760
Sorry... I guess I got argumentative again.

00:11:32.300 --> 00:11:36.080
I'll be going now, Yang. 
Thanks for seeing me off.

00:11:38.970 --> 00:11:40.300
Take care.

00:11:40.810 --> 00:11:44.270
Get further promoted to make 
up for Jean Robert, too.

00:11:58.130 --> 00:11:59.910
Welcome home, Commodore.

00:11:59.910 --> 00:12:01.380
Thanks, Julian.

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:04.890
I was beginning to think you 
wouldn't be coming home,

00:12:04.890 --> 00:12:06.070
but I'm glad you did.

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:09.140
I made some of the Irish stew you like.

00:12:09.140 --> 00:12:12.150
That makes me glad I came home hungry.

00:12:12.150 --> 00:12:14.300
What made you think that, though?

00:12:14.300 --> 00:12:17.400
Rear Admiral Cazerne contacted me.

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:22.900
He told me you ran out of the ceremony 
hand-in-hand with a pretty woman.

00:12:23.850 --> 00:12:25.500
That jerk...

00:12:28.050 --> 00:12:30.920
Your future plans? It's that time already?

00:12:30.920 --> 00:12:35.430
No, but there are some students who get 
enough credits to graduate half a year early.

00:12:35.790 --> 00:12:40.020
I see... And are you planning 
to become a soldier?

00:12:40.280 --> 00:12:42.980
Yes. I'm a soldier's son, after all.

00:12:43.420 --> 00:12:47.420
There's no law that says a son has 
to inherit his father's job.

00:12:47.750 --> 00:12:50.730
My father was a trader, in fact.

00:12:50.730 --> 00:12:55.400
But if I don't, you'll have to repay 
the loan you took to raise me.

00:12:55.400 --> 00:12:56.460
I'll repay it.

00:12:57.910 --> 00:13:01.030
Don't underestimate your guardian.

00:13:01.030 --> 00:13:03.030
I have enough saved up for that.

00:13:03.030 --> 00:13:06.790
Besides, you don't need 
to graduate that early.

00:13:06.790 --> 00:13:08.840
Why not enjoy life a bit longer?

00:13:08.840 --> 00:13:11.260
I couldn't trouble you that much!

00:13:11.510 --> 00:13:14.210
Don't get smart. You're still a child.

00:13:14.210 --> 00:13:18.740
Children are supposed to live 
by leeching off their parents.

00:13:19.210 --> 00:13:22.140
Thank you, but...

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:31.650
Is it in style lately for groups of clowns 
like those to visit people's homes?

00:13:31.650 --> 00:13:33.500
They're the Patriotic Knight Corps.

00:13:33.760 --> 00:13:36.450
I don't know any circus troupe like that.

00:13:36.450 --> 00:13:38.960
They're a radical nationalist group.

00:13:39.550 --> 00:13:44.960
They've recently become notorious for 
harassing people who express anti-war views.

00:13:45.530 --> 00:13:49.180
It's strange, though. 
Why would they come to our house?

00:13:54.930 --> 00:13:56.610
Commodore Yang!

00:13:56.610 --> 00:14:00.490
We of the Patriotic Knight 
Corps condemn you!

00:14:00.930 --> 00:14:02.830
Out of arrogance over your own exploits,

00:14:02.830 --> 00:14:07.440
you have disturbed the unity of our navy 
and compromised its will to fight.

00:14:08.360 --> 00:14:10.400
I know you're aware of what you've done.

00:14:11.810 --> 00:14:15.460
You disgraced a sacred memorial service!

00:14:16.030 --> 00:14:20.020
When everyone in attendance responded 
to the chairman's impassioned speech

00:14:20.020 --> 00:14:21.950
by swearing to defeat the Empire,

00:14:22.340 --> 00:14:25.150
you alone refused to stand

00:14:25.150 --> 00:14:29.840
and behaved in a way that mocked 
the citizens' determination!

00:14:30.410 --> 00:14:34.150
We condemn your arrogance!

00:14:34.150 --> 00:14:37.470
If you have anything to say, 
step outside and face us.

00:14:38.120 --> 00:14:40.580
Did you really do that?

00:14:40.580 --> 00:14:42.080
Yeah, sort of...

00:14:42.080 --> 00:14:43.520
But why?!

00:14:43.520 --> 00:14:44.870
Even if you were opposed,

00:14:44.870 --> 00:14:48.380
you could have just stood and clapped, 
and everything would be fine!

00:14:48.380 --> 00:14:51.410
Others only see how you react 
on the outside, after all!

00:14:51.830 --> 00:14:55.120
You sound a lot like Rear Admiral Cazerne.

00:14:55.430 --> 00:14:58.200
You don't have to bring him into this.

00:14:58.200 --> 00:15:01.100
Even a child could have told you that!

00:15:01.100 --> 00:15:04.590
If you won't come out, 
we have our own ideas!

00:15:05.860 --> 00:15:06.830
Get down!

00:15:12.850 --> 00:15:15.700
Julian, which one is the 
switch for the sprinklers?

00:15:15.700 --> 00:15:19.030
It's 2-A-4. What are you going to do?

00:15:19.030 --> 00:15:23.230
I just need to teach those 
guys some manners.

00:15:23.610 --> 00:15:24.810
Oh...

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:29.140
What's the problem? 
Why don't you say something?

00:15:29.610 --> 00:15:32.300
If you don't respond, 
prepare for another—

00:15:42.350 --> 00:15:43.730
Hey! All of you—

00:15:53.880 --> 00:15:58.310
Now do you see how scary things can 
get when you anger a gentleman,

00:15:58.310 --> 00:16:00.430
you cowardly bunch of thugs?

00:16:03.940 --> 00:16:05.310
I'll help with that.

00:16:05.310 --> 00:16:08.270
No, you would actually just get in the way.

00:16:08.270 --> 00:16:11.850
I know. Why don't you sit 
on that table there?

00:16:11.850 --> 00:16:14.130
The table? Come on...

00:16:20.840 --> 00:16:22.600
This is my Wanli porcelain.

00:16:23.480 --> 00:16:27.770
The one thing my father left me 
that was actually genuine...

00:16:30.470 --> 00:16:34.120
<i>Hey there, son. Could you put 
your guardian on for me?</i>

00:16:34.120 --> 00:16:35.210
He's over there.

00:16:38.080 --> 00:16:42.830
<i>Were you always into sitting 
on tables in your own house?</i>

00:16:42.830 --> 00:16:44.780
Depends on the day of the week.

00:16:44.780 --> 00:16:48.300
Well, forget that. There's a matter 
that demands immediate attention.

00:16:48.980 --> 00:16:52.180
<i>I need you to report to the 
Integrated Tactical Operations Center ASAP.</i>

00:16:52.180 --> 00:16:53.610
Right now?

00:16:53.950 --> 00:16:56.640
<i>It's a direct order from Chief Sithole.</i>

00:16:57.080 --> 00:16:59.480
What does the chief want with me?

00:16:59.880 --> 00:17:03.280
<i>All I know is that it's urgent.</i>

00:17:03.280 --> 00:17:05.090
<i>I'll see you there later.</i>

00:17:13.750 --> 00:17:15.930
It looks like I'll be out late.

00:17:16.400 --> 00:17:17.930
You can go on to bed.

00:17:17.930 --> 00:17:19.380
Yes, Commodore.

00:17:19.960 --> 00:17:24.380
Julian, I think we'll be able to 
laugh off what happened tonight.

00:17:24.870 --> 00:17:28.810
But before too long, that might 
not be possible anymore.

00:17:30.100 --> 00:17:33.770
It looks like the times 
are slowly turning bad.

00:17:34.160 --> 00:17:39.060
Commodore, I may be speaking out of turn,

00:17:39.060 --> 00:17:41.230
but please don't worry about that.

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:45.460
I want you to walk the path 
you believe is right.

00:17:46.110 --> 00:17:50.020
I believe you are right, above all others.

00:18:05.450 --> 00:18:08.350
I see you haven't gotten 
any better at chess.

00:18:08.350 --> 00:18:13.990
So, did you call me out here 
at this hour to play chess?

00:18:13.990 --> 00:18:15.290
Of course not.

00:18:14.730 --> 00:18:18.450
<u>Alliance Navy Integrated Tactical Operations Center Chief
</u>Fleet Admiral Sidney Sithole

00:18:15.630 --> 00:18:18.450
You're here because I needed 
to inform you of something.

00:18:18.900 --> 00:18:22.010
Officially, the personnel 
change is set for tomorrow,

00:18:22.010 --> 00:18:25.930
but you're going to be 
promoted to Rear Admiral.

00:18:25.930 --> 00:18:28.450
This is a formal decision, 
not a tentative one.

00:18:28.450 --> 00:18:30.660
You know why you're being 
promoted, don't you?

00:18:31.190 --> 00:18:33.010
Because I lost, right?

00:18:33.010 --> 00:18:35.490
You really haven't changed a bit.

00:18:35.860 --> 00:18:39.220
You say the most scathing things 
with such a benign expression.

00:18:39.220 --> 00:18:41.720
Just like when you were a cadet.

00:18:41.720 --> 00:18:45.830
But that is the truth, 
is it not, Headmaster...

00:18:45.830 --> 00:18:48.320
I mean, Chief?

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:50.500
What makes you think that?

00:18:50.900 --> 00:18:54.560
Ancient military science texts say that 
the conferment of more rewards

00:18:54.560 --> 00:18:56.350
is a sign of desperation.

00:18:56.350 --> 00:19:00.290
They say it's done out of a need to 
distract attention from a loss.

00:19:00.290 --> 00:19:03.120
In a sense, you're right.

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:06.080
After our biggest loss in recent history,

00:19:06.080 --> 00:19:09.270
both our servicemen and 
our citizens are shaken.

00:19:09.270 --> 00:19:13.280
What we need to calm them is a hero.

00:19:13.280 --> 00:19:16.000
That means you, Rear Admiral Yang.

00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:21.830
I suppose it wasn't your wish 
to be a man-made hero, was it?

00:19:23.050 --> 00:19:25.030
Let's change the subject...

00:19:25.030 --> 00:19:30.290
to the strategy you proposed to Vice 
Admiral Paetta before the battle began.

00:19:30.290 --> 00:19:34.670
Do you think we would have won 
if that had been implemented?

00:19:34.670 --> 00:19:35.990
Yes, I think so.

00:19:36.350 --> 00:19:41.470
But might it not be possible to utilize 
that strategy at another time?

00:19:41.470 --> 00:19:46.280
When that time comes, perhaps we can 
have revenge on Count Lohengramm.

00:19:46.930 --> 00:19:49.270
If this victory has made him arrogant,

00:19:49.270 --> 00:19:54.290
and he cannot resist the temptation to take 
on a massive force with a smaller one again,

00:19:54.290 --> 00:19:57.330
perhaps the strategy I proposed 
could be of use again.

00:19:58.120 --> 00:19:59.220
But...

00:19:59.590 --> 00:20:00.690
But?

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:04.660
But it's unlikely that will happen.

00:20:05.120 --> 00:20:09.630
Defeating a larger force with a 
smaller one seems grand at first,

00:20:09.980 --> 00:20:12.630
but it strays from common 
tactical practice.

00:20:12.630 --> 00:20:16.260
Rather than war strategy, it falls into 
the category of sleight of hand.

00:20:16.680 --> 00:20:19.910
I'm sure Count Lohengramm knows that.

00:20:20.490 --> 00:20:24.000
He'll most likely attack with an 
overwhelmingly large force next.

00:20:25.060 --> 00:20:27.480
I see. You're probably right.

00:20:28.610 --> 00:20:30.360
Incidentally, one more thing...

00:20:30.360 --> 00:20:33.150
And this is a tentative 
decision, not a formal one.

00:20:33.150 --> 00:20:36.260
There'll be a change to 
part of our formation.

00:20:36.570 --> 00:20:41.560
The remnants of the 4th and 6th Fleets 
will be reinforced with more men

00:20:41.560 --> 00:20:44.520
and combined to make the 13th Fleet.

00:20:44.520 --> 00:20:48.720
You will most likely be appointed as 
the first commander of that fleet.

00:20:49.040 --> 00:20:53.940
Isn't command of a fleet only given to 
those ranked Vice Admiral or higher?

00:20:53.940 --> 00:20:57.900
The new fleet will be about 
half the size of the others.

00:20:58.430 --> 00:21:01.980
And the 13th Fleet's first mission will be...

00:21:01.980 --> 00:21:05.160
the capture of Iserlohn Fortress.

00:21:05.900 --> 00:21:11.080
You mean we are to capture 
Iserlohn with half a fleet?

00:21:11.080 --> 00:21:12.220
Correct.

00:21:12.870 --> 00:21:15.860
Have you been listening 
to anything I've said?

00:21:15.860 --> 00:21:19.580
Of course. I'm saying this based on that.

00:21:20.220 --> 00:21:22.410
You believe it's possible?

00:21:22.410 --> 00:21:27.710
I believe that if you 
can't do it, no one can.

00:21:29.600 --> 00:21:31.410
Do you think you can't do it?

00:21:33.150 --> 00:21:39.570
If you successfully lead this new fleet 
in the capture of Iserlohn Fortress,

00:21:39.920 --> 00:21:45.540
Defense Committee Chairman Trunicht will 
be forced to recognize your talents,

00:21:45.540 --> 00:21:48.900
regardless of how he feels 
about you personally.

00:21:52.390 --> 00:21:54.280
I will give it my best effort.

00:21:56.220 --> 00:21:59.200
The chief has about 70 
days left in his term.

00:21:59.600 --> 00:22:02.290
Obviously, he's hoping 
to win a second term,

00:22:02.290 --> 00:22:08.580
which means the capture of Iserlohn 
will have to be completed by then.

00:22:09.210 --> 00:22:14.790
If I'm successful, the chief's standing 
with Chairman Trunicht is reinforced.

00:22:14.790 --> 00:22:17.020
He's certainly a cunning man.

00:22:17.020 --> 00:22:20.040
The chief is a saint compared 
to that bastard.

00:22:20.040 --> 00:22:23.640
The guy who's behind that 
Patriotic Knight Corps is...

00:22:23.640 --> 00:22:26.900
Chairman Trunicht himself, right?

00:22:26.900 --> 00:22:28.650
I knew that right away.

00:22:33.950 --> 00:22:36.440
Rest while you can. You'll be busy soon.

00:22:36.770 --> 00:22:38.190
Honestly...

00:22:38.880 --> 00:22:41.730
Why would anyone ever want 
to become a soldier?

00:22:50.460 --> 00:22:52.880
No matter how much you complain,

00:22:52.880 --> 00:22:55.160
the navy won't ever let go of you.

00:22:55.160 --> 00:22:57.490
Be ready, Yang Wen-li.

00:22:58.820 --> 00:23:00.650
Thank you, sir.

00:23:14.580 --> 00:23:18.510
I guess I won't get to drink the 
tea Julian makes for a while.

00:24:50.010 --> 00:25:00.010
<b>Episode 6</b>

00:24:50.010 --> 00:25:00.010
<b>The Capture of Iserlohn (Part 1)</b>

00:24:50.450 --> 00:24:54.850
<i>Next time on </i>Legend of the 
Galactic Heroes - Die Neue These<i>,</i>

00:24:55.320 --> 00:24:59.830
<i>episode six, "The Capture 
of Iserlohn (Part 1)."</i>
